# =============================================================================
# LINE ENDINGS
# =============================================================================
#
# This file exists because of a failure that looked like nothing at all.
#
# `.prettierrc` sets `endOfLine: "lf"` and `npm run verify` starts with
# `format:check`, so LF is not a preference here — it is the build gate. Git on
# Windows defaults to `core.autocrlf=true`, which rewrites every text file to
# CRLF *on checkout*. With no `.gitattributes` to say otherwise, the two
# disagree, and the disagreement is invisible until the moment it is not:
#
#   * `git diff` shows nothing, because autocrlf normalises back to LF when git
#     compares the working tree against the index. The repository content is
#     correct the whole time.
#   * Nothing is wrong immediately after writing a file, because the editor
#     wrote LF and git has not touched it.
#   * Then somebody switches branch. Git re-materialises every file that
#     differs — through the CRLF filter — and 119 files that nobody edited are
#     suddenly rejected by `format:check`, with an empty `git diff` to explain
#     it. On this repository that happened during a routine merge to `main`.
#
# `eol=lf` makes the checkout itself produce LF regardless of `core.autocrlf`,
# so the working tree matches what the gate demands on every platform, and no
# contributor has to configure their machine to be able to run `npm run verify`.
# =============================================================================

* text=auto eol=lf

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary — never transformed, never diffed as text
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `text=auto` already detects these by content. Naming them is belt and braces:
# content detection reads only the first blob of a file, and a corrupted image
# is a defect nobody notices until a page renders wrong in production.

*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.avif  binary
*.mp4   binary
*.webm  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.pdf   binary
